  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \n\nA strong anticyclone is expected to persist near the SoCal coast\nthrough at least Thursday, which will keep temperatures well above\nnormal and near daily records across SoCal. Temperatures are also\nexpected to warm another 1-2 degrees across south-central AZ on\nThursday, following the passage of a weak shortwave trough. Even\nsouth-central AZ will see high temperatures up to 10 degrees above\nnormal (99-102 degrees) on Thursday. An Extreme Heat Watch \nremains in effect through Thursday for Imperial Valley, CA and \nYuma, AZ, where probabilities of Major HeatRisk are highest \n(50-70%). This watch is likely to be upgraded to a warning tonight\nor tomorrow morning and Friday is being considered for the heat\nalert as well. Latest NBM forecast shows 30-50% Major HeatRisk \nprobs on Friday for the same areas under the current watch. The \ncoastal anticyclone is expected to breakdown heading into Friday \nas the synoptic pattern shifts, with a Pacific low moving toward \nthe CA coast, but low level temperatures are expected to remain \nvery warm. Latest global ensembles show 850mb temps cooling by \nonly a degree or two centigrade into Friday, which should only\nresult in 1-4 degrees of surface cooling from Thursday. Latest\nhigh temperature forecast for Friday has 101-106 degrees across\nsoutheast CA and southwest AZ to 97-101 degrees across south-\ncentral AZ.\n\nAs alluded to, the pattern will shift heading into the end of this\nweek and this weekend with a trend toward troffing and negative\nheight anomalies across the Western CONUS. While there is good\nconfidence in the trough pattern developing, there is still a lot\nof model uncertainty/spread regarding how the trough pattern will\nevolve heading into the end of the weekend and start of next week.\nIt does look like the Pacific low that will near the CA coast on\nFriday will dip south this weekend and interact or faze with TC \nRachel while at the same time a trough will move out of the Gulf\nof Alaska and strengthen as it moves south down the northern\nPacific coast. There are certainly a lot of eyes on what will\nhappen with TC Rachel and whether or not it will help bring rain\nto the Desert Southwest, just like with TCs Odalys and Polo early\nlast week. Latest guidance has trended toward an eventual turn to\nthe northeast by Rachel by the end of the weekend. While this\ntrend does lend itself in favor of a push of moisture toward the\nSouthwest CONUS, there is still a lot of uncertainty in the\nforecast this far out, especially with timing and evolution of the\ntrough moving down the northern Pacific coast and how that may\naffect the remnants of Rachel. Current thinking is at least mid \nand high level moisture will eventually shear off or Rachel up \ntoward the Southwest, which may bring showery activity as early \nas Sunday and then the more potent coastal trough/low will help\ndrive additional shower and storm chances across AZ, while also\nsending a cold front and cooler temperatures through the region\nearly next week. Again uncertainty is still high and it is ill-\nadvised to buy into single deterministic long-range model runs\nthat may show a strong tropical circulation pushing into the \nU.S.. Stay tuned for forecast updates through the week.",
